                <fr:title text="Binius">Binius</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    A proof system built from the ground up on <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002L/" title="Binary Tower Fields" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002L/" display-uri="dhsorens-002L" type="local">binary tower fields</fr:link>, with <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0035/" title="Small-Field Commitments and Ring Switching" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0035/" display-uri="dhsorens-0035" type="local">ring switching</fr:link> as the mechanism that keeps small values cheap. The most complete demonstration of the small-field argument taken to its limit.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>The stack: tower fields, multilinear polynomials, a small-field commitment, and sumcheck-based constraint checking.</html:li>
        <html:li>How a bit-level computation avoids paying for embedding into a large field.</html:li>
        <html:li>Flock, which specialises the same idea to large batches of one Boolean circuit and is what <fr:link href="/dhsorens-004H/" title="Poseidon, and Why Ethereum Walked Away" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-004H/" display-uri="dhsorens-004H" type="local">made conventional hashes cheap enough to prove</fr:link> that Ethereum dropped Poseidon.</html:li>
        <html:li>Where the extension field is still required, and what fraction of the work it accounts for.</html:li>
        <html:li>The measured comparison against 31-bit prime systems, and on which workloads it holds.</html:li>
        <html:li>ArkLib's <html:code>ProofSystem/Binius</html:code> and <html:code>RingSwitching</html:code>: what is stated and what is proved.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
