            <fr:title text="Univariate and Multilinear Polynomials">Univariate and Multilinear Polynomials</fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>
    Two representations of the same data, supporting different arguments. The univariate world pairs with low-degree testing; the multilinear world pairs with <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002Q/" title="The Sumcheck Protocol" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002Q/" display-uri="dhsorens-002Q" type="local">sumcheck</fr:link>. Knowing which one a protocol lives in explains most of its shape.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>The multilinear extension of a function on the Boolean hypercube; uniqueness; the equality polynomial and its role.</html:li>
        <html:li>Degree versus number of variables as the size parameter, and what each buys.</html:li>
        <html:li>Why FRI wants univariate and Basefold, HyperPlonk, and Binius want multilinear.</html:li>
        <html:li>How <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003C/" title="WHIR" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003C/" display-uri="dhsorens-003C" type="local">WHIR</fr:link> unifies the two, and what the translation costs.</html:li>
        <html:li>Computable representations — dense, sparse, coefficient, evaluation form — and which operations each makes cheap. This is <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0041/" title="CompPoly" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0041/" display-uri="dhsorens-0041" type="local">CompPoly</fr:link>'s design problem.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
